  "published": "2026-08-13T15:08:44.69Z",
  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "A runner outlives the world it runs. `ArchWorldRuntime.Shutdown` destroyed the world and left every\nsystem registered, so the next `Initialize` added a second full set to the same runner — and the one\nafter that a third. Any owner that starts more than one session in a process hits it: a node that runs\na match, a level, or a raid.\n\nNothing throws and nothing is logged. Every copy updates, so the world simulates several times per\ntick, and the stale copies still hold the state they were left with. A predictor keeps applying the\nlast input of the previous session for as long as the process lives, walking the player in the\ndirection they were moving when it ended. It was reported as a physics bug, then a network bug, then a\nstuck key, and it was none of them.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- `ArchSystemRunner.Shutdown` now clears its registrations after shutting each system down, so a\n  runtime that is initialized again starts from the systems its callback registers and no others.\n  `ArchSystemRunner.Count` is zero after a shutdown, which is the assertion worth making in a game\n  that reuses a world.",
